          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Cicero</strong><br>\nMarcus Tullius Cicero was born in 106 BC in Arpinum, Italy. He came from a wealthy municipal family of the equestrian order and received a quality education in Rome. To further his studies, Cicero traveled to Athens, Greece, where he studied philosophy and law. This education laid the foundation for his future career as a statesman and orator.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Cicero's Career as an Orator</strong><br>\nCicero is often regarded as one of the greatest Roman orators. He began his career as a lawyer and gained fame for his speeches in court. His oratory skills were marked by his sharp wit, clear explanations, and persuasive arguments. Cicero's speeches were not only means of legal defense or prosecution but also became a form of political communication.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Cicero's Political Life</strong><br>\nCicero's political career was as remarkable as his oratory. He climbed Rome's political ladder, becoming consul in 63 BC. As consul, he faced the Catiline Conspiracy, a plot to overthrow the Roman Republic. Cicero's handling of the crisis, including his famous Catilinarian Orations, cemented his reputation as a defender of the republic.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
