          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Philosophy and Writings</strong><br>\nBesides politics and law, Cicero was a prolific writer and philosopher. He wrote on a variety of subjects, including ethics, politics, and philosophy. His philosophical texts aimed to make Greek thought accessible to Romans, thus greatly influencing Roman education and culture.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Cicero and Julius Caesar</strong><br>\nCicero's relations with Julius Caesar were complex. He admired Caesar's military genius but disapproved of his disregard for the republic's traditional limits on power. After Caesar's assassination, Cicero hoped to restore the republic, but the political landscape had irrevocably changed.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Proscriptions and Death</strong><br>\nThe aftermath of Caesar's death led to the rise of the Second Triumvirate, which initiated a wave of proscriptions, a list of individuals marked for execution. Cicero, an outspoken critic of Mark Antony and supporter of the republic, was placed on this list. He was captured and killed in 43 BC.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
